The Max-Flow Min-Cut Theorem, beyond its elegant theoretical foundation, serves as a remarkably versatile tool for solving a wide array of combinatorial optimization problems. Its power lies in the ability to transform seemingly disparate problems into a network flow formulation, where the maximum flow value directly corresponds to the optimal solution of the original problem. This section delves into one of its most prominent applications: **Maximum Bipartite Matching**, and briefly touches upon other significant uses in graph algorithms.

