So, we can state:
$|f| = \sum_{u \in S, v \in T} f(u, v) \le \sum_{u \in S, v \in T} c(u, v)$

The right-hand side of this inequality is exactly the definition of the capacity of the cut $(S, T)$.
Thus, for any flow $f$ and any cut $(S, T)$:
$|f| \le c(S, T)$

This crucial inequality implies that the maximum possible flow value must be less than or equal to the capacity of *any* s-t cut. Consequently, the maximum flow value must also be less than or equal to the capacity of the *minimum* s-t cut. This establishes an upper bound for the maximum flow.

